I'm learning probe mechanism and notice there is chance for replication starting from an index less than match index in probe state, unlike that in replicate state. For example, a rejected probe response got delayed and is received when the leader falls into another probe state. This is a rare case of cause.

I'm not sure if this is by design. But to my understanding, allowing such a smaller index implies we want to tolerate loss of already replicated log entries. 

```
func (pr *Progress) MaybeDecrTo(rejected, matchHint uint64) bool {
	if pr.State == StateReplicate {
		// The rejection must be stale if the progress has matched and "rejected"
		// is smaller than "match".
		if rejected <= pr.Match {
			return false
		}
		// Directly decrease next to match + 1.
		//
		// TODO(tbg): why not use matchHint if it's larger?
		pr.Next = pr.Match + 1
		return true
	}

	// The rejection must be stale if "rejected" does not match next - 1. This
	// is because non-replicating followers are probed one entry at a time.
	if pr.Next-1 != rejected {
		return false
	}

	pr.Next = max(min(rejected, matchHint+1), 1)
	pr.MsgAppFlowPaused = false
	return true
}
```

I deployed a 9 replica setup of ETCD in a distributed setting.

Then I ran an attack script that partitions the leader in a simplex manner 

```
func () Attack() {
	start_time := time.Now()

	for time.Now().Sub(start_time).Seconds() < float64(duration) {
		// select the leader
		node_id := getLeader()
		// set all incoming links to leader to have high loss
		time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)
		// reset all configured losses
		time.Sleep(3 * time.Second)
	}
}
``` 

In the above attack, the leader can send AppendEntries to all replicas, but no replica sends back votes to leader.

I observe that the current leader is not changed (no leader election).

Reason -- Because the leader is sending the AppendEntires, the followers don't trigger the timeout.

Proposed Fix -- followers should time out not only when they don't hear timeouts, but also when nothing is committed for a long time.

